e  function table and  the other argument so this is  pretty useful however it eats the stack  pretty badly  every time you call another function  you're adding something to the stack and  you never get that back  so when we use swears are in production  we just try not to think about this too  much  other interesting things this version of  map is is really interesting uh we're  doing the stick of thing in a vector and  pull it back out tactic here  but inside the vector we have a back  quoted  hash map literal and at the syntactic  level this has two things in it  but if you're calling this function with  say eight things four keys and four  values  what you're gonna end up doing is is  unquoting the first key  as the key in this map and the other  seven things  as the value and it somehow expands out  and you get the correct hash map so  that's really weird  um also you can get a gen sim just for  fun  and and this expression computes a  constant gen sim so every time you call  it you get the same one so it's not very  useful as a gen sim but if you like  looking at gen sims  you can get them if you have this this  function literal in several places  you'll get a different gen sim in each  place that you create it but  also tim implemented uh rest  which is a useful function to have uh  this basically does some sort of weird  recursive thing with a vector where it  walks through the whole thing to build  up the rest of it  i  yeah so it's a little tedious but it  works  um so what are what are the limits of  what we can do um is it turing complete  the first thought everybody has is well  it's just lame to calculus right  well that's not so obvious actually  because we can't nest things they're not  really closures  so i don't think there's any  straightforward reduction there however  i've become about 98  convinced that we can implement the ski  calculus if we want to  i would have done it myself but i don't  have time so  please let me know how that goes  um i